{
	title: 'Full-screen terminal across tabs',
	description: `Switching tabs behaves as tasks/cc/terminal.md says (Height management, Tab switching, Force repaint). In full mode the prompt, status and tab bar are always on the terminal's last rows: blank rows after a short history pad the frame to the screen height, from the focused tab, rows and chrome alone, never from what this process painted before (a `peak` high-water mark put the prompt mid-screen after a restart on a short tab). Having two or more tabs permanently enters full mode, at the first paint that sees them (so a restart on tab 2 clears the last run's frame instead of painting under it), regardless of how tall the focused tab is, as the old Hal decided to keep tab heights simple; single-tab use keeps grow mode and the shell's own scrollback. In full mode every tab switch, Ctrl-L and resize is a canonical force repaint: clear scrollback with CSI 3J, then write the focused tab's whole frame, every history line, never a viewport slice. Output of tabs not shown never paints. The emergency keys and the last frame on quit keep their rules (tasks/README.md).
Test with the terminal emulator in render.test.ts: after switching between a 3-line tab and a 200-line tab, scrollback plus screen hold exactly the focused tab's lines; a fresh renderer on a short tab (a restart) paints the prompt on the bottom rows, and it stays there across switches to shorter, empty and taller tabs and across a resize. a single-tab session never writes CSI 3J; with two tabs the first paint is canonical; output arriving in a background tab changes nothing on screen.`,
